{"text": " Buyers who didn't ask the price are being replaced by buyers who do. America's own central bank said the same thing. The desk that runs the Federal Reserve's bond portfolio told the committee that ownership of Treasury securities had shifted from relatively price-insensitive official sector holders to more price-sensitive private investors, and that this could have implications for the term premium. And the term premium is just the new buyer's extra charge on top for tying their money up for 30 years instead of 30 days. The United Kingdom's Office for Budget Responsibility also found a similar thing happening to their government bonds, known as guilts. Today, British-defined benefit pension funds hold guilts worth about 27% of the entire British economy. They estimate that falls to under 6% over the long term, and shifts this size cause damage. It's estimated that weaker pension demand could push the interest rate on British government debt up by around 80 basis points, which would cost the British government about 22 billion pounds per year. So this is a global trend we're seeing that ripples through every major economy. It's not that investors refuse to lend governments money anymore. We're still seeing auctions clear. It's that the buyers governments once knew and loved got replaced by buyers who care about the price they pay and they want a better price.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 3.66, "text": " Buyers who didn't ask the price are being replaced by buyers who do.", "tokens": [50363, 11763, 364, 508, 1422, 470, 1265, 262, 2756, 389, 852, 6928, 416, 14456, 508, 466, 13, 50551], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10754957118955981, "compression_ratio": 1.6666666666666667, "no_speech_prob": 0.01344594918191433, "words": [{"word": " Buyers", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.4, "probability": 0.7093276679515839}, {"word": " who", "start": 0.4, "end": 0.54, "probability": 0.9867545962333679}, {"word": " didn't", "start": 0.54, "end": 